    "mod.pocket.tag": "12 activities · select to expand",
    "act.breath.name": "Forest Breathing",
    "act.breath.purpose": "Anchor physiological regulation to the forest environment. Structured breathwork (4-4-6) conducted outdoors leverages phytoncide inhalation and the auditory environment to deepen parasympathetic activation more effectively than indoor equivalents.",
    "act.breath.lookfor": "Visible slowing of breath, uncrossing of arms, lowering of shoulders. Some participants will spontaneously close their eyes. The shift from chest to abdominal breathing is a key indicator of regulation.",
    "act.breath.example": "\"Place your feet flat and feel the ground. Breathe in for four counts — try to breathe what the trees are exhaling. Hold for four. Out for six. We do this together, here, in this air.\"",
    "act.bark.name": "Bark Rubbing & Texture Journal",
    "act.bark.purpose": "Use tactile contact with trees as a grounding intervention. The physical act of pressing paper to bark and rubbing integrates touch, proprioception, and sight simultaneously, activating somatic presence and supporting dissociation recovery.",
    "act.bark.lookfor": "Slowing of movement, increased quiet focus, and often spontaneous emotional commentary. The texture journal becomes a material artefact for later reflection or clinical discussion.",
    "act.bark.example": "\"Choose three trees. Press your paper gently against the bark and rub the crayon across it. Then write one word beside each rubbing — the word the texture gives you. Don't think. Write the first thing.\"",
    "act.deadwood.name": "Dead Wood Dialogue",
    "act.deadwood.purpose": "Use fallen or dead wood as a projective object for exploring endings, loss, and transformation. Dead wood in forest ecology is not waste — it is habitat and nutrient source. This reframe supports grief, burnout, and life-transition work.",
    "act.deadwood.lookfor": "Spontaneous projections — \"this looks like my last job\", \"it's falling apart but there's still life in it\". Resistance to endings may manifest as avoidance of the dead wood or intellectualisation. Sit with it.",
    "act.deadwood.example": "\"Find a piece of dead wood — fallen, decaying, or broken. Sit with it for a few minutes. What does it remind you of? What's still alive in it, if anything?\"",
    "act.mandala.name": "Forest Mandala",
    "act.mandala.purpose": "Invite intentional, meditative creation using foraged natural materials arranged in a circular pattern on the ground. The process slows cognition, focuses attention, and externalises internal structure. The mandala is not kept — releasing it is part of the practice.",
    "act.mandala.lookfor": "The structure chosen (tight/loose, symmetrical/asymmetrical, dominated by one material) reflects current internal organisation. Anxiety often produces rigid symmetry. The moment of releasing the mandala can be emotionally significant.",
    "act.mandala.example": "\"Collect whatever the forest offers — you have fifteen minutes. Then arrange what you've gathered in a circle on the ground. There's no right way. When you're done, we'll look together before letting it go.\"",
    "act.track.name": "Tracking & Noticing",
    "act.track.purpose": "Develop observational patience and decentred attention by following signs of non-human life — tracks, scat, feathers, chewed bark, disturbed ground. This practice shifts participants out of self-referential rumination by directing curiosity outward.",
    "act.track.lookfor": "Increased patience and willingness to slow down. The shift from \"I don't see anything\" to genuine curiosity is therapeutically significant. Participants with hypervigilance may find tracking a useful redirect of an overactive threat-detection system.",
    "act.track.example": "\"We're looking for signs that something was here before us. Tracks, gnawed wood, nests, pellets, disturbed leaves. You're not looking for the animal — you're reading what it left. Slow down. Get low if you need to.\"",
    "act.weather.name": "Weather Dialogue",
    "act.weather.purpose": "Use prevailing weather conditions as a projective screen for emotional states. Whatever the weather actually is — rain, fog, sun, wind — becomes a resource for dialogue. The activity normalises emotional variability by anchoring it to natural cycles rather than pathology.",
    "act.weather.lookfor": "Projection richness — \"it's grey like I feel\", \"the wind is my anxiety\". The spontaneity of the metaphor is the data. Also note when participants refuse the weather as metaphor — that refusal is itself material.",
    "act.weather.example": "\"Look up. What's the weather doing right now? If this weather were your emotional state, what would that mean? What weather would you want instead?\"",
    "act.roots.name": "Roots & Branches",
    "act.roots.purpose": "Use a living tree as a structural metaphor for personal foundations and future directions. Participants reflect on what roots them (values, relationships, memories, place) and what they are reaching toward (goals, healing, connection).",
    "act.roots.lookfor": "Which half participants engage with more readily — roots or branches — reveals orientation toward past or future. Difficulty naming roots may indicate attachment disruption or identity fragmentation.",
    "act.roots.example": "\"Stand next to a tree you like. Look at its roots — what holds it. Look at its branches — where it's going. In your journal: what are your roots? What are your branches?\"",
    "act.pairwalk.name": "Silent Paired Walk",
    "act.pairwalk.purpose": "Develop relational attunement through sustained shared silence and non-verbal presence. Pairs walk together without speaking for a defined period. The post-walk debrief explores what was noticed, shared, and left unsaid — often revealing relational patterns.",
    "act.pairwalk.lookfor": "Whether pairs maintain proximity or drift apart. Who sets the pace and whether the other accommodates. The pressure to speak — and which partner breaks first — is diagnostic. In couple or family work this becomes a rich relational map.",
    "act.pairwalk.example": "\"Walk together for the next twenty minutes without speaking. You don't need to point things out or explain. Just walk and notice. Let the forest be enough. We'll talk about it when we return.\"",
    "visual.sitspot": "Radiating awareness — sound, light, movement from a single fixed point",
    "visual.map": "Felt-sense qualities annotated onto a personal spatial map of the forest",
    "visual.walk": "A defined route with a symbolic midpoint — the threshold between states",
    "visual.council": "Circle of participants, each paired with a chosen tree; the speaking turn orbits the group",
    "visual.breath": "4-count inhale · 4-hold · 6-count exhale — synchronised with forest atmosphere",
    "visual.bark": "Tactile imprint of bark transferred to paper — a somatic record of contact",
    "visual.deadwood": "Decay as generative metaphor — endings create the conditions for new growth",
    "visual.mandala": "Natural materials in concentric rings — outer world to inner centre, then released",
    "visual.track": "Following evidence of invisible life — what was here, what passed through, what remains",
    "visual.weather": "Current emotional weather vs. desired state — externalised in the sky above",
    "visual.roots": "Roots = what grounds and sustains · Branches = directions of growth and aspiration",
